# datelite

A small, dependency-free library for parsing date and time strings into standard values.

`datelite` takes the messy date strings you meet in logs, CSV files, and user input (`"2026-10-20"`, `"20/10/2026"`, `"Oct 20, 2026 3:15 PM"`) and turns them into a single, predictable result. It does not try to be a calendar, a scheduler, or a timezone database. It parses, and it tells you clearly when it can't.

## Features

- Parses ISO 8601 dates and times, plus a set of common human-written formats.
- Explicit handling of day/month ordering (`"01/02/2026"` is ambiguous; you choose).
- Returns a `Date` or `DateTime` value, or raises a `ParseError` that says what went wrong.
- No runtime dependencies.
- Small surface area: one main function and a handful of options.

## Installation

```sh
npm install datelite
```

> Adjust the install command to your package manager and language. The examples below use JavaScript.

## Quick start

```js
import { parseDate } from "datelite";

parseDate("2026-10-20");
// { year: 2026, month: 10, day: 20 }

parseDate("2026-10-20T15:30:00Z");
// { year: 2026, month: 10, day: 20, hour: 15, minute: 30, second: 0, offset: "Z" }

parseDate("Oct 20, 2026");
// { year: 2026, month: 10, day: 20 }
```

## Supported formats

| Input                        | Kind      | Notes                                |
| ---------------------------- | --------- | ------------------------------------ |
| `2026-10-20`                 | date      | ISO 8601                             |
| `2026-10-20T15:30:00Z`       | datetime  | ISO 8601, UTC                        |
| `2026-10-20T15:30:00+02:00`  | datetime  | ISO 8601, with offset                |
| `20 Oct 2026`                | date      | Day, abbreviated month, year         |
| `Oct 20, 2026`               | date      | Month, day, year                     |
| `20/10/2026`                 | date      | Requires `dayFirst` (see below)      |
| `10/20/2026`                 | date      | Requires `dayFirst: false` if ambiguous |
| `2026-10-20 15:30`           | datetime  | Space instead of `T`, no seconds     |

Month names are matched in English. Other locales are not supported yet.

## API

### `parseDate(input, options?)`

Parses a single string.

**Parameters**

- `input` (`string`): the text to parse.
- `options` (`object`, optional):
  - `dayFirst` (`boolean`, default `false`): when a numeric date is ambiguous (for example `01/02/2026`), read the first number as the day. Ignored when the input is unambiguous.
  - `strict` (`boolean`, default `false`): when `true`, only ISO 8601 input is accepted.

**Returns**

A plain object. Date fields are always present. Time fields are present only when the input includes a time.

| Field    | Type     | Always present | Description                            |
| -------- | -------- | -------------- | -------------------------------------- |
| `year`   | `number` | yes            | Four-digit year                        |
| `month`  | `number` | yes            | 1–12                                   |
| `day`    | `number` | yes            | 1–31                                   |
| `hour`   | `number` | no             | 0–23                                   |
| `minute` | `number` | no             | 0–59                                   |
| `second` | `number` | no             | 0–59                                   |
| `offset` | `string` | no             | `"Z"` or `"+HH:MM"` / `"-HH:MM"`       |

Time fields are omitted entirely when the input has no time. The library does not invent `00:00`, so you can tell "midnight" apart from "no time given".

**Throws**

`ParseError` if the input is not a recognised date, or if the date is impossible (for example `2026-02-30`).

### `tryParseDate(input, options?)`

Same as `parseDate`, but returns `null` instead of throwing. Use it when bad input is expected, such as when reading user-supplied data.

```js
import { tryParseDate } from "datelite";

const d = tryParseDate(userInput);
if (d === null) {
  showError("Please enter a valid date.");
}
```

### `ParseError`

The error thrown by `parseDate`.

- `message` (`string`): a human-readable description.
- `input` (`string`): the original input.

## Examples

### Ambiguous day and month

```js
parseDate("03/04/2026");
// { year: 2026, month: 3, day: 4 }   (default: month first)

parseDate("03/04/2026", { dayFirst: true });
// { year: 2026, month: 4, day: 3 }
```

### Strict ISO mode

```js
parseDate("20 Oct 2026", { strict: true });
// throws ParseError: expected ISO 8601 format
```

### Handling errors

```js
import { parseDate, ParseError } from "datelite";

try {
  const d = parseDate(line);
} catch (err) {
  if (err instanceof ParseError) {
    console.warn(`Skipping "${err.input}": ${err.message}`);
  } else {
    throw err;
  }
}
```

## What this library does not do

- **Timezone conversion.** `datelite` reads offsets but does not convert between zones or apply daylight saving rules.
- **Date arithmetic.** No adding days, computing differences, or ranges.
- **Relative dates.** `"next Tuesday"` and `"3 days ago"` are rejected.
- **Locales other than English month names.**

For these, pair the parsed values with a dedicated date library.

## Development

```sh
git clone https://github.com/your-org/datelite.git
cd datelite
npm install
npm test
```

Pull requests are welcome. Please include tests for any new format, and for any input that should be rejected.

## License

MIT. See [LICENSE](LICENSE).

