## Arduino (any board)

```cpp
// Blink the built-in LED once per second (500 ms on, 500 ms off)

const int LED_PIN = LED_BUILTIN;  // Pin 13 on most Arduino boards

void setup() {
  pinMode(LED_PIN, OUTPUT);
}

void loop() {
  digitalWrite(LED_PIN, HIGH);  // LED on
  delay(500);
  digitalWrite(LED_PIN, LOW);   // LED off
  delay(500);
}
```

### Non-blocking variant
This version doesn't stall the CPU, so you can do other work in `loop()`:

```cpp
const int LED_PIN = LED_BUILTIN;
const unsigned long HALF_PERIOD_MS = 500;

unsigned long lastToggle = 0;
bool ledState = false;

void setup() {
  pinMode(LED_PIN, OUTPUT);
}

void loop() {
  unsigned long now = millis();
  if (now - lastToggle >= HALF_PERIOD_MS) {  // Safe across millis() rollover
    lastToggle += HALF_PERIOD_MS;            // Avoids drift
    ledState = !ledState;
    digitalWrite(LED_PIN, ledState);
  }
  // ... other tasks can run here ...
}
```

## Bare-metal AVR (ATmega328P, 16 MHz)

```c
#define F_CPU 16000000UL
#include <avr/io.h>
#include <util/delay.h>

int main(void) {
    DDRB |= (1 << DDB5);          // PB5 (Arduino pin 13) as output

    while (1) {
        PORTB ^= (1 << PORTB5);   // Toggle LED
        _delay_ms(500);
    }
}
```

Build and flash with avr-gcc:
```bash
avr-gcc -mmcu=atmega328p -Os -o blink.elf blink.c
avr-objcopy -O ihex blink.elf blink.hex
avrdude -c arduino -p m328p -P /dev/ttyACM0 -b 115200 -U flash:w:blink.hex
```

**Notes:**
- "Once per second" here means one full on/off cycle per second. If you want the LED to turn *on* once per second with a short flash, use something like 100 ms on and 900 ms off.
- If you're using an external LED, connect it through a current-limiting resistor (about 220–330 Ω) between the pin and the LED's anode, with the cathode to GND.

